#heading(level:2,"Basic ergonomics")
#para([#text("Choose a solid, level table with room for the stand, keyboard, mouse pad, and cables. Place the screen at a comfortable viewing angle, the keyboard directly in front of you, and the mouse beside it. Keep light from reflecting directly off the display. Adjust your chair rather than spending the afternoon leaning toward the computer.")])
#para([#text("Leave room to move your hands and take occasional breaks. Put speakers and their stands where they will not tip, block the screen, or pull on the computer’s cables. A small computer still deserves a sensible place to sit.")])
#pagebreak(weak:true)
#heading(level:2,"Putting it all together")
#subhead("The stand, keyboard and mouse")
#instruction(1,[#strong[#text("Open the collapsible stand and place it on the level table.")] #text("Seat the support leg in its final position, as the arrow in the illustration shows. Check that it is stable before adding the computer.")])
#instruction(2,[#strong[#text("Place the PiForma on the stand and center it.")] #text("Check that it rests securely rather than leaning toward one side.")])
#wide-illustration("1-4a", "1-4a.png", "Seat the support leg in the position indicated by the arrow, then check the stand before adding the computer.", width:200.000pt)
#instruction(3,[#strong[#text("Put the keyboard directly in front.")] #text("Turn it on with the switch near its top-right edge.")])
#instruction(4,[#strong[#text("Put the mouse on its pad beside the keyboard.")] #text("Its power switch is underneath; turn it on if necessary.")])
#para([#text("The mouse can ordinarily remain on between uses because it enters a low-power idle state. You can still switch it off for transport or prolonged storage. This does not switch off the keyboard, which has its own control and Bluetooth connection.")])
#para([#text("The stand illustration deliberately shows one support leg short of its final position. Move it into the seat indicated by the arrow before placing the PiForma on top. The partly assembled position is not the position for supporting the computer.")])
#subhead("The keyboard controls")
#para([#text("The overhead view identifies the keyboard's volume control and nice!view status display. The USB charging port and power switch are in the indicated area near the top-right edge; their labels show the general location because the port and switch themselves are not visible from above. The USB-C port sits on the right, where the keyboard’s ADB port used to be; charge the keyboard there.")])
#para([#text("Caps Lock deserves a warning. It is a vintage locking key, and on the PiForma it behaves like a Shift key held down. While it is down, letters come out as capitals and the number keys type symbols. Make sure Caps Lock is up, level with Shift and Option, before you start and before you pack the keyboard away.")])
#wide-illustration("1-4b", "1-4b.png", "Keyboard controls. The USB port and power-switch callouts indicate their general location, not a directly visible socket or switch.", width:366.000pt)
#subhead("The mouse controls")
#para([#text("Use the left button for an ordinary click and the wheel for scrolling. The other button is the right button, although the illustration is maintaining a respectable air of mystery. Perhaps it is a second left click. We have never heard of a right click.")])
#para([#text("The Sync button is not needed in the normal setup: this mouse is used with its one receiver. Leave it alone for ordinary use.")])
#wide-illustration("1-4c", "1-4c.png", "The mouse buttons, scroll wheel, and Sync button. The second button is awaiting official recognition.", width:244.252pt)
#para([#text("Underneath are the battery compartment, power switch, and optical tracking window. The mouse runs on one AA battery; it is not recharged. The illustration calls that last item the Eye Correction Laser. Its job is to follow the mouse pad, not improve your eyesight. Keep it pointed at the desk; no eye treatments are included.")])
#wide-illustration("1-4d", "1-4d.png", "Mouse underside: battery compartment, power switch, and optical tracking sensor.", width:273.714pt)
#subhead("Power for a longer session")
#para([#text("If the internal battery is charged, you do not need a power cord for a short session. A full charge typically lasts about six hours of general use, or about four hours of emulated games, Nintendo 64 games especially. For longer sessions, keep the PiForma connected to external power when it is available.")])
#para([#text("The desktop has no battery gauge, and there is no low-battery warning. Check the battery display through the top slits now and then. When the battery runs out, the PiForma stops at once, just as if the power switch had been turned off, and unsaved work is lost. After a full drain, connect external power and allow a minute or two before it will start.")])
#para([#text("Take the power cable from the brown pouch. The original rear AC inlet supplies the internal charger. The battery can charge while the computer’s power switch is off. Use the cable and charging arrangement intended for this build.")])
#subhead("USB devices and controllers")
#para([#text("Use the rear USB port for the USB hub when you need more than one external USB connection. The two Nintendo 64 controllers are in the accessory pouch. Chapter 3 explains how to approach the game launcher and its controls. Use only the leads or adapters supplied for these controllers; do not force a connector into the USB hub.")])
#subhead("Bose speakers and stands")
#para([#text("The Bose speakers are an optional extra, not part of the PiForma package; they do not fit in the bag. The pair shown are 1984 Bose RoomMate speakers. They are almost as large as the PiForma’s screen, which makes for a great deal of sound from a very small picture, and they were chosen because their proportions match.")])
#para([#text("Connect the speakers to the rear audio output socket. Switching sound between the internal speaker and the rear socket is not yet a menu choice; ask the builder if it needs changing. Give the speakers a stable place beside the working area, with cables out of the way and no strain on the PiForma. Their stands should not make the table or the computer’s folding stand unstable.")])
#wide-illustration("1-5", "complete-setup.png", "The complete setup: the PiForma on its stand, the keyboard in front, the mouse on its pad, a Bose speaker on its stand at each side and a Nintendo 64 controller at the left.", width:263.115pt)
#subhead("Bringing sound into the PiForma")
#para([#text("The external audio adapter brings sound from an external source into the PiForma over USB so it can play through the normal speakers. This is external audio into the computer, not the computer’s ordinary audio output. Use the adapter intended for this setup. The rear RCA jack carries composite video; it is not an audio input.")])
#heading(level:2,"Important safety instructions")
#para([#text("Keep liquids away, protect the display, and leave ventilation openings clear. Use a stable stand on a level table. Do not carry the computer by cables or press on the screen to move it.")])
#para([#text("The power switch does not disconnect external charging power from the internal charger. Switching the computer off alone does not make internal electrical work safe. Do not disassemble it with charging power connected. Internal repairs and battery disconnection belong with someone familiar with this particular build, not in a first-start procedure.")])
#para([#text("Stop using the computer if it becomes unusually hot, smells hot, or has damaged power wiring or a damaged battery. Do not keep retrying power-on in that condition. Use the intended charging equipment. This privately built device has no claimed electrical-safety certification.")])
#heading(level:2,"Starting up the computer")
#annotated("The startup chime",[#text("A silent startup does not by itself mean that the computer has failed. Use the green light and display as well as the chime when checking what has happened.")],[
#instruction(1,[#strong[#text("Find the physical power control on the right side and switch the PiForma on.")]])
#instruction(2,[#strong[#text("Look for the green light at the front.")]])
#instruction(3,[#strong[#text("Listen for a startup chime.")] #text("Depending on the rear selector’s position, there may be no sound.")])
#instruction(4,[#strong[#text("Watch for the startup picture or sequence.")] #text("The desktop normally follows in about one to two minutes; allow for some variation.")])
])
#subhead("Checking the battery")
#para([#text("If nothing happens, check the battery before assuming something has broken. The outside plunger mechanically presses the button on the internal battery pack, showing its charge indicators; pressing it may also wake or enable the pack.")])
#para([#text("The normal, management-approved way to read the battery is to press the rear battery plunger to turn on its display, then peer through the slits in the top to see the percentage. You do not have to open the compartment for this check.")])
#wide-illustration("1-6a", "1-6b.png", "Read the battery percentage through the top slits after pressing the rear battery plunger. The eye shows where to look.", width:330.000pt)
#para([#text("Press the plunger as intended, look at the battery indication, and try the right-side power control again. If the battery is empty, apparently asleep, or refuses to start despite showing charge, connect the intended external power supply and try again.")])
#para([#text("The owner has sometimes recovered a start after roughly a minute on external power, even when the battery previously showed full. Try a brief connection before taking anything apart, but do not count on one minute as a fixed recovery time. This is an observed oddity, not an established diagnosis.")])
#subhead("If there is no green light")
#para([#text("If there is no green light, no chime, and no picture, start with the physical power control, battery indication, plunger, and intended power cable. Retry the startup sequence after the battery check or brief external-power connection. These observations do not yet separate every power fault from every display fault. If they do not help, stop short of internal troubleshooting and consult the builder.")])
#subhead("If the light is on but there is no chime")
#para([#text("Some positions on the rear chime-selector dial are intentionally silent or empty. With the PiForma powered on, move the dial through several positions in either direction and listen for an available sound, as the owner suggests.")])
#para([#text("If it never sounds, there may be a problem with the chime module, its speaker, or its power, but silence alone is not a diagnosis. The startup chime uses its own sound hardware; normal desktop audio is a separate path. Internal checks should be made only by someone familiar with the build and with power safely disconnected.")])
#subhead("If the light and chime work but the screen stays blank")
#para([#text("Allow time for the startup picture. Check that the display source is set for the Raspberry Pi’s HDMI connection. If the screen remains blank, the display side of the system needs attention; a chime does not prove that the Pi has completed its boot.")])
#para([#text("Do not disturb internal connections while the machine is powered; ask the builder to investigate a persistent blank or distorted picture. Appendix B provides other checks by symptom.")])
#subhead("If the desktop is slow to appear")
#para([#text("Give the computer approximately one to two minutes to reach the desktop. Boot time can vary. If it remains at the startup picture or never reaches a usable desktop, record what you see rather than repeatedly interrupting power.")])
